          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Marriage and bride imagery signifies the covenantal union of God or Christ with his people, especially Christ with the Church, characterized by fidelity, love, and final consummation.\",\"book_summary\":\"Marriage signifies the covenant union of God and his people as a one-flesh bond, with the wife receiving the Husband's name and covering; divorce is its opposite.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Marriage/bride = the covenant union of God or Christ with his people, especially the Church.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Marriage/bride = the covenant union of God with his people.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book's broader wording, “God and His people,” accommodates both the Old Testament Israel-wife imagery and the New Testament Christ-Church imagery recognized by the consensus.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The one-flesh and covering language elaborates the nature of the same covenant union rather than replacing its referent.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum identification, both equate marriage or bride imagery with the covenant bond between God/Christ and his people. The book adds details about one flesh, name, covering, and divorce, but these do not materially alter the symbol's referent or extension.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts strongly support covenant, covering, one-flesh union, Christ and the Church, and the Lamb's marriage, but they do not explicitly support the specific claim that the wife receives the Husband's name. That detail is therefore less directly grounded than the core identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
